"""Front matter, Introduction and Research Methods (numbers that depend on results are not used here)."""

TITLE = "Predictive Reliability of Knowledge Tracing Models Under Sparse and Noisy Student Response Data"
AUTHORS = "Meixian Jin\nEmporia State University\nmjin1@g.emporia.edu"
KEYWORDS = ("Knowledge Tracing; Predictive Reliability; Probability Calibration; Sparse Response Histories; "
            "Response Noise; Educational Artificial Intelligence")

INTRO = [
    ("h1", "1. Introduction"),
    ("p", "A knowledge tracing (KT) model is rarely asked only to rank learners. In an adaptive practice system its output, the probability that the next response is correct, decides what is reviewed, which exercise comes next, or whether another assessment is requested (Abdelrahman et al., 2022; Pelánek, 2017). Anyone acting on a prediction of 0.90 is relying on it being wrong about one time in ten. Yet most KT evaluation still reports discrimination, usually the area under the ROC curve (AUC), on benchmark collections (Liu et al., 2022; Pelánek, 2015). Discrimination and calibration are different properties. A model can order responses well and still be systematically overconfident or timid, and AUC will not show it (Guo et al., 2017; Niculescu-Mizil & Caruana, 2005)."),
    ("p", "The models usually compared make different bets. Bayesian knowledge tracing (BKT) treats mastery of a skill as a latent binary state, updated after every response and tied to answers through guess and slip parameters (Corbett & Anderson, 1995). Item response theory (IRT) assumes a stable ability and fixed item difficulties (Pelánek, 2017). Deep knowledge tracing (DKT) hands the problem to a recurrent network (Piech et al., 2015), and AKT adds attention with a monotonic distance decay and Rasch-type question embeddings (Ghosh et al., 2020). Whether the deep models win depends on the data (Gervet et al., 2020; Khajah et al., 2016), which is a reason to doubt that any single ranking of models travels well."),
    ("p", "Two ordinary conditions make the calibration question harder. Histories are short whenever a learner is new or a session is brief. And responses are a noisy signal: guessing and slipping limit what a few answers can reveal, and they are hard to disentangle from the latent state itself (Baker et al., 2008; Beck & Chang, 2007). Logs may also carry outright wrong labels (Frénay & Verleysen, 2014). Elsewhere in machine learning, predicted probabilities are known to degrade under dataset shift even when ranking holds up (Ovadia et al., 2019). How far that carries over to KT predictors, and to which of them, is not settled."),
    ("p", "Uncertainty in KT is not a neglected topic. UKT represents interactions as distributions and adds a contrastive loss aimed at aleatory noise (Cheng et al., 2025); Dynamic LENS keeps epistemic uncertainty through variational encoders and Bayesian state-space updates (Christie et al., 2024); and selective prediction with Monte Carlo dropout lets a model defer when it is least certain (Mitton et al., 2026). Uncertainty-aware and anomaly-aware designs have also been proposed for resource scheduling in LLM serving and service meshes (Gu et al., 2026; Tao, 2026); those address a different task and data, and we cite them only as examples of attention to model reliability when predictions drive automated decisions. This paper proposes no new algorithm. It audits the probabilities of standard KT models under controlled sparsity and noise, tests one simple post-hoc correction, and claims no priority for the general observation that KT predictions can be unreliable."),
    ("p", "One distinction matters for everything below. Estimating a knowledge state is not the same as predicting the next response. BKT’s mastery probability, IRT’s ability parameter, and the hidden units of DKT and AKT are different objects, and real logs provide no labelled version of any of them. We therefore score every model on the one quantity they share, the observed next response, and we do not evaluate latent-state recovery. Attention weights and hidden units are not read as reasons for a learner’s errors."),
    ("p", "Three questions guide the study."),
    ("p", "**RQ1.** How does limited response history affect predictive discrimination and probability calibration across knowledge tracing models?", False),
    ("p", "**RQ2.** How do guessing, slipping, and corrupted historical labels affect high-confidence prediction errors?", False),
    ("p", "**RQ3.** Can simple post-hoc calibration improve reliability, and when do its benefits fail to transfer across data conditions?", False),
    ("p", "Learners are simulated from two generators with different assumptions, so no model is tested only on data built from its own assumptions. Every number reported here comes from code run for this study. No real data were analysed, and nothing below is evidence about real learners. What the paper offers is a reproducible protocol and a set of observations on how discrimination, calibration, and high-confidence errors behave when histories are short or noisy."),
]

METHODS = [
    ("h1", "2. Research Methods"),
    ("h2", "2.1. Data Sources and Simulation Design"),
    ("p", "**Data mode.** This study uses simulated data only; no real dataset was read, analysed, or reported. Public logs such as ASSISTments (Feng et al., 2009) or EdNet (Choi et al., 2020) require checks of version, licence, and fields and are left to replication. All tables and figures derive from predictions saved by the code run for this paper."),
    ("p", "Each replicate contains $N=3000$ learners, each answering $T=60$ items from a bank of 200 items grouped into 10 skills of 20 items. The skill sequence stays on the previous skill with probability 0.6 and otherwise switches to a random skill, which produces practice runs; the item is drawn uniformly within the skill. Five independent replicates (seeds 1–5) each generate a new item bank and new learners. A separate development seed (9001) is used only for hyperparameter selection. Learners are split 60/15/10/15 into training, validation, calibration, and test sets (1,800/450/300/450)."),
    ("p", "**Synthetic-KT-A** is a two-state learning, forgetting, guessing, and slipping process. For learner $i$ and skill $k$ the latent state $z_{ik}\\in\\{0,1\\}$ is updated over time. The response to item $j$ follows"),
    ("eq", r"P(y_{it}=1\mid z_{it}=1)=1-s_j,\qquad P(y_{it}=1\mid z_{it}=0)=g_j,", 1),
    ("p", "where $s_j$ is the probability of an incorrect answer when the skill is mastered and $g_j$ the probability of a correct answer when it is not. These are generator parameters, not causes observable in a log. Item-level values vary around the nominal $g$ and $s$ on the logit scale with standard deviation 0.3. Initial mastery has logit $\\operatorname{logit}(0.35)+u_i+\\nu_k+e_{ik}$ with $u_i\\sim N(0,0.7^2)$, $\\nu_k\\sim N(0,0.5^2)$, $e_{ik}\\sim N(0,0.3^2)$. After each attempt an unmastered skill becomes mastered with probability $\\sigma(\\operatorname{logit}(0.18)+0.5u_i+\\omega_k)$, $\\omega_k\\sim N(0,0.3^2)$. Between two attempts at the same skill separated by $\\Delta$ interactions, a mastered skill is forgotten with probability $1-e^{-\\lambda\\Delta}$, $\\lambda=0.006$. These values give visible learning curves and moderate accuracy (proportion correct about 0.63 for KT-A and 0.62 for KT-B at the target events); they were not estimated from any population."),
    ("p", "**Synthetic-KT-B** is a dynamic, multidimensional IRT process whose assumptions differ from BKT. Ability follows $\\theta_{it}=\\alpha_i+\\nu_{i,k_t}+\\beta_i\\,t/(T-1)+\\varepsilon_{it}$, with learner ability $\\alpha_i\\sim N(0,1)$, skill-specific offsets $\\nu_{ik}\\sim N(0,0.5^2)$, growth $\\beta_i\\sim N(1,0.4^2)$, and an AR(1) disturbance ($\\phi=0.9$, innovation SD 0.15). The response probability is"),
    ("eq", r"P(y_{it}=1)=g_j+(1-g_j-s_j)\,\sigma\!\left(a_j(\theta_{it}-b_j)\right),", 2),
    ("p", "with $\\log a_j\\sim N(0,0.3^2)$ and $b_j\\sim N(0,1)$. Here $g_j$ and $s_j$ are the lower and upper asymptotes of the response curve. They play the same formal role as response randomness in KT-A but are not the same psychological mechanism, so the two generators are analysed separately and never pooled. Ability drift, multidimensionality, and item discrimination violate the assumptions of BKT and of a static-ability 2PL model."),
    ("p", "Response randomness is varied through five generating configurations per mechanism: a centre configuration ($g=0.20$, $s=0.15$), two values of $g$ (0.10 and 0.30, with $s=0.15$), and two values of $s$ (0.05 and 0.25, with $g=0.20$). Other generator parameters were not varied, which is a limitation noted in Section 4."),
    ("h2", "2.2. Prediction Task and Model Specifications"),
    ("p", "The task is next-response prediction. Given the visible history, a sequence of (item, skill, response) triples that precede the target event, and the target item, a model outputs $p=P(y=1)$. To make every model see the same events, targets are fixed to events 50–59 of each test learner, giving ten target events per learner and 4,500 per replicate."),
    ("p", "Five predictors are compared. (i) The *Baseline* is a smoothed item-level correct rate, $p_j=(c_j+\\alpha\\bar p)/(n_j+\\alpha)$, which uses no history. (ii) *BKT with forgetting* has one parameter set per skill (initial mastery, learning, forgetting, guess, slip) fitted by expectation–maximisation on training learners (up to 60 iterations from three starting points, keeping the best likelihood), with guess and slip bounded above and forgetting bounded at 0.3; the prediction filters the visible same-skill responses starting from the prior. (iii) *2PL-IRT* has item discrimination and difficulty fitted by maximum a posteriori estimation on training learners (priors: ability $N(0,1)$, log-discrimination $N(0,\\sigma_a^2)$, difficulty $N(0,2^2)$); for a test learner the ability is re-estimated from the visible window only, with item parameters held fixed. (iv) *DKT* embeds the (skill, response) token, passes the sequence through one GRU layer (Cho et al., 2014), and outputs a probability per skill, of which the target skill’s entry is used (Piech et al., 2015). (v) The *AKT-style* model uses Rasch-type question embeddings, one attention head over earlier interactions with scores multiplied by $\\exp(-\\vartheta\\,\\mathrm{distance})$ for a learnable $\\vartheta>0$, and a two-layer prediction head (Ghosh et al., 2020). It is simplified relative to the original AKT (no encoder stack, temporal rather than context-aware distance) and is therefore called AKT-style."),
    ("p", "No deep learning framework was available, so DKT and the AKT-style model were implemented on NumPy with reverse-mode automatic differentiation; gradients matched finite differences (maximum relative error about $10^{-5}$). Training used Adam (Kingma & Ba, 2015), gradient-norm clipping at 5, weight decay $10^{-5}$, batch size 64, at most 40 epochs, and early stopping with patience 5 on validation loss. Hyperparameters came from small predefined grids (two to six configurations per family) evaluated on the validation learners of the development seed (Table 1; selected values for each mechanism are listed there). The simulated bank contains every item in the training data, so cold-start behaviour is not exercised. Items with several skills are not generated here; real logs would need a rule for assigning them, which changes the model inputs."),
    ("table1",),
    ("h2", "2.3. Sparsity and Noise Conditions"),
    ("p", "Information sparsity, response randomness, and record errors are varied separately. **History length**: for each target, the visible history is the most recent $L\\in\\{3,5,10,20,50\\}$ events in their original order. Target events are identical for every $L$. **Label corruption**: each visible history response is flipped independently with probability $\\eta\\in\\{0,0.05,0.10,0.20\\}$ using common random numbers, so that flips are nested across $\\eta$ and identical across models. Only the visible history is corrupted; the target label used for scoring stays clean. **Response randomness**: guess and slip values vary at generation as described in Section 2.1 and therefore affect training, validation, and test data alike."),
    ("p", "Two settings are kept apart. In the *test-only shift* setting, models are fitted once on clean, full-length training histories (60 events) and evaluated under the shifted conditions; the perturbation occurs only in the test (and calibration) windows. In the *matched training* setting, the two neural models are retrained under the evaluation condition: for history length, the loss is computed only at the last position of randomly drawn windows with exactly $L$ history events (6,000 windows per epoch, target positions anywhere from $L$ to 59); for label corruption, the input histories are flipped during training (windows with $L=50$) while the loss targets remain clean. Baseline, BKT, and IRT have global parameters fitted on clean training data in every condition, so matched-versus-test-only contrasts are limited to the neural models."),
    ("h2", "2.4. Training, Calibration, and Evaluation Protocol"),
    ("p", "Splits are by learner, responses are ordered by time, and only earlier events enter a history; the target answer and later answers are never visible. Hyperparameter selection and early stopping use training and validation learners only, and all models share the same splits. The calibration learners (10%) are used for post-hoc calibration only; the test learners (15%) are used for reporting only. Figure 1 summarises the workflow."),
    ("fig1",),
    ("p", "Temperature scaling is used as a simple post-hoc reference. A predicted probability $p$ is replaced by $p'=\\sigma(\\operatorname{logit}(p)/T)$ with a single $T>0$ fitted by minimising the negative log-likelihood on the calibration learners (bounded search of $\\log T$ over $[-2.5,2.5]$). Two uses are distinguished. In *target-environment recalibration*, $T$ is fitted on calibration windows with the same $L$ and $\\eta$ as the test windows. In *direct transfer*, $T$ is fitted on clean windows with $L=50$ and applied unchanged to shifted test windows. Matched-trained models are recalibrated in the target environment only. A positive temperature is monotone, so AUC should not change; this was verified (Section 3.4)."),
    ("h2", "2.5. Reliability Metrics and Statistical Analysis"),
    ("p", "Let $p_i$ be the predicted probability of a correct response and $y_i\\in\\{0,1\\}$ the observed response for $N$ target events. The primary scores are the Brier score and the negative log-likelihood (NLL):"),
    ("eq", r"\mathrm{Brier}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}(p_i-y_i)^2,", 3),
    ("eq", r"\mathrm{NLL}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left[y_i\log p_i+(1-y_i)\log(1-p_i)\right].", 4),
    ("p", "In computing NLL, probabilities are clipped to $[10^{-6},1-10^{-6}]$. ROC-AUC is reported as the discrimination measure. The expected calibration error (Naeini et al., 2015) uses $B=10$ equal-width bins of the predicted probability of a correct response:"),
    ("eq", r"\mathrm{ECE}=\sum_{b=1}^{B}\frac{n_b}{N}\left|\bar y_b-\bar p_b\right|,", 5),
    ("p", "where $n_b$, $\\bar y_b$, and $\\bar p_b$ are the count, observed proportion correct, and mean prediction in bin $b$. Reliability diagrams plot $\\bar y_b$ against $\\bar p_b$, omitting bins with fewer than 50 pooled events. ECE is positively biased in finite samples and depends on the binning, so its sensitivity was checked with 5, 15, and 20 equal-width bins and with 10 equal-mass bins (Nixon et al., 2019), and small ECE differences are interpreted cautiously."),
    ("p", "For high-confidence errors, the predicted class is $\\hat y_i=\\mathbb{1}[p_i\\ge 0.5]$ and the confidence is $c_i=\\max(p_i,1-p_i)$. Among predictions with $c_i\\ge 0.90$ we report the error proportion together with the coverage (their share of all predictions) and the count; the error proportion is not available (NA) when no prediction qualifies. A smaller number of high-confidence predictions is not treated as lower error risk."),
    ("p", "Uncertainty is quantified with learner-level resampling (Efron, 1979): the 450 test learners, with their ten target events each, are resampled with replacement, and for pooled results the per-replicate bootstrap distributions (400–500 resamples) are averaged across the five replicates before the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles are taken. Replicate-to-replicate variability is reported as the standard deviation across the five seeds. Paired differences between models, conditions, or calibration variants use identical resampled learners, and the ten target events of one learner are never treated as independent. No significance tests or *p* values are reported; five replicates are too few for them to be informative."),
]
